    "lawCode": "Add Art 27-L §2799-u, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4497\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public health law, in relation to\nestablishing the rare disease advisory council\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo amend the public health law, in relation to establishing the rare\ndisease advisory council\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nThis act amends the public health law by adding a new article 27-L to\nread as follows:\n\nSection 1 adds a new section 2799-u to the public health law to estab-\nlish a rare disease advisory council within the department with the duty\nto advise and consult on policy matters related to rare disease. It\nestablishes the number and type of members on the council, the appoint-\nment process, the meeting schedule, and advisory council duties.\n\nSection two provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn the United States, a rare disease is defined as a condition that\naffects fewer than 200,000 people. Nearly 7,000 rare diseases currently\ndo not have a recommended treatment plan and this significantly impacts\nthe lives of those living with rare diseases, their family members, and\ntheir communities.\n\nWhile New Yorkers currently benefit from a range of services including\nrare disease screening for infants, mandated medical food coverage and\nprescription cost sharing, there are many opportunities for improvement.\nInvesting in research now can later lead to the development of treat-\nments and cures.\n\nRare disease advisory councils exist to provide recommendations related\nto rare disease research and treatment. In other states including\nConnecticut, North Carolina, Pennsylvania, Illinois, and Alabama, rare\ndisease advisory councils have already been established and operate to\ninform each state about the impact of rare disease on its citizens and\nto develop strategies for improvement of the people living with rare\ndiseases.\n\nThis act establishes the rare disease advisory council in New York to\nadvise and consult on policy matters related to rare disease. It defines\nthe number and makeup of the body of council members and the scope of\nresponsibilities for members.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATION:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
